---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import ApiName from '@/components/ApiName.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Emphasis from '@/components/motion/scenes/Emphasis.astro';
import { MotionEmphasisReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-emphasis.tsx';
import { MotionEmphasisAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-emphasis.ts';
import MotionEmphasisSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-emphasis.svelte';

const defineBadgePop = `import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// Grows to 135 % and comes back, on the fast spatial spring.
defineMotion('badge-pop', {
  verb: 'emphasize',
  keyframes: [{ scale: 1 }, { scale: 1.35 }, { scale: 1 }],
  timing: 'spring.fast.spatial',
});`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="emphasis"
  title="Motion: draw attention"
  description="Replay a short emphasis on an element, in HTML, React, Angular and Svelte: a press on a button, or a shake on a field whose value is refused."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Change <code>data-motion-state</code> on an element and its emphasis replays: a press on a button, or a shake on a field whose value is refused.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Call <code>play()</code> from <code>useMotion</code> and the emphasis replays: a press on a button, or a shake on a field whose value is refused.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Bind <code>[udxMotionState]</code> to a value, and each change replays the emphasis: a press on a button, or a shake on a field whose value is refused.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Pass <code>state</code> in the options of <code>motion()</code>, and each change replays the emphasis: a press on a button, or a shake on a field whose value is refused.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <MotionStage scene="emphasis" label="Live example: Draw attention" height={280}>
    <Emphasis />
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>Replay an emphasis</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      Put <code>data-motion</code> on the element with the name of an emphasis, and call <code>connectMotion()</code> once
      for the page. Then change <code>data-motion-state</code> each time the element must draw attention: any new value replays
      the emphasis. A count works well, such as the number of clicks for a press, or of refusals for a shake.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      Call <code>useMotion()</code> with the name of an emphasis, put its <code>ref</code> on the element, and call its
      <code>play()</code> each time the element must draw attention: on each click for a press, when a value is refused
      for a shake.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">
      Add <code>Motion</code> to your component's <code>imports</code>, put <code>udxMotion</code> on the element with the
      name of an emphasis, and bind <code>[udxMotionState]</code> to a value that changes each time the element must draw
      attention: any new value replays the emphasis. A count works well, such as the number of clicks for a press, or of
      refusals for a shake.
    </p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      Attach <code>motion()</code> to the element with the name of an emphasis, and pass <code>state</code> in its options:
      <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('press', () =&gt; (&#123; state: count &#125;))&#125;</code>. Change the value each time
      the element must draw attention: any new value replays the emphasis. A count works well, such as the number of clicks
      for a press, or of refusals for a shake.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>
  <p>
    The example uses two presets: <code>press</code> dips to 96 % and comes back, and <code>shake</code> moves 6 px from
    side to side. To move a component of the library, put the motion on a plain element around it, as the example does.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-emphasis" previewHeight={[224, 216]}>
    <MotionEmphasisReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionEmphasisAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionEmphasisSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Replay it another way</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">
      To replay the emphasis without changing <code>data-motion-state</code>, call <code>getMotion(element, 'emphasis')?.play()</code>,
      from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.
    </p>
    <p slot="react">
      To replay the emphasis each time a value changes, render the element as a <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> and pass the
      value as <code>state</code>: <code>&lt;Motion as="span" name="press" state=&#123;count&#125;&gt;</code>. To replay it
      from a handler, give it a <code>motionRef</code> and call <code>motionRef.current?.play()</code>.
    </p>
    <p slot="angular">
      To replay the emphasis without changing a value, take the directive in a template reference and call its
      <code>play()</code>: <code>&lt;span udxMotion="press" #press="udxMotion"&gt;</code>, then
      <code>press.play()</code>.
    </p>
    <p slot="svelte">
      To replay the emphasis without changing a value, take the element with <code>bind:this</code> and call
      <code>getMotion(element, 'emphasis')?.play()</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion</code>.
    </p>
  </ForFramework>

  <h2>Define your own emphasis</h2>
  <p>
    Define a motion with the verb <code>emphasize</code>: its keyframes play forward on each replay. End them where they
    start, so that the element comes back to rest. The badge of the live example grows to 135 % and comes back:
  </p>
  <Code data-code={defineBadgePop} data-language="ts" />
  <p>
    Define it once, in a module that runs before the element is bound, then attach it by its name, as you would a preset:
    <ApiName
      html={'data-motion="badge-pop"'}
      react="useMotion('badge-pop')"
      angular={'udxMotion="badge-pop"'}
      svelte="motion('badge-pop')"
    />.
  </p>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <p>
    When a replay comes while the previous one still plays, <code>interrupt</code> decides: <code>restart</code> plays it
    again from the first frame, and <code>carry</code> goes on from where the element is.
  </p>
  <MotionOptions verb="emphasize" />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>
    <code>press</code> plays on the fast spatial spring, the default of an emphasis. <code>shake</code> sets its own
    timing, a linear curve of 400 ms, so each of its five moves takes 80 ms and reaches its full 6 px.
  </p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.fast.spatial', 'duration.medium4', 'easing.linear']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <p>
    Under reduced motion, or with <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor, an emphasis does not
    move: the element shows its end frame at once. So do not let the motion alone say what happened: in the live example,
    the refused field is marked <code>aria-invalid</code> and shows its error. See
    <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If the element must also enter and leave, give it a presence motion as well: an element holds one motion of each
      kind, such as
      <ApiName
        html={'data-motion="slide press"'}
        react={'<Motion name="slide press">'}
        angular={'udxMotion="slide press"'}
        svelte="motion('slide press')"
      />.
    </li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
